LEARN.add({"subject":"investing","lessons":[{
"id":"inv-d2",
"subject":"investing",
"level":3,
"order":14,
"type":"deep",
"minutes":60,
"title":"How the economy moves markets",
"hook":"Rates, inflation, jobs, earnings: learn the machine behind the headlines and read the morning news like a pro.",
"tags":["interest rates","inflation","federal reserve","business cycle","yield curve","recession","jobs report","earnings"],
"blocks":[
{"t":"chapter","title":"The machine behind the headlines","minutes":6},
{"t":"p","text":"Meet **Daniel**. He is 46, a finance director at a mid-size company in Miami, and he has about $400,000 for retirement in a simple 60/40 portfolio of broad index funds. Every morning on his commute he reads the market news. Every morning he sees headlines like “Stocks fall as strong jobs data lifts yields” and thinks: _why would good news about jobs be bad news for stocks?_"},
{"t":"p","text":"This masterclass answers that question and many like it. You already know the 101 and 201 tools: risk and return, diversification, bonds, allocation, behavior. Now we connect them to the big machine that moves everything: the economy. We will follow one fictional economic cycle across six years, chapter by chapter, and watch what it does to Daniel’s portfolio."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"timeline","events":[{"when":"Year 1","label":"Recovery begins"},{"when":"Years 2 to 3","label":"Steady expansion"},{"when":"Year 4","label":"Overheating, rate hikes"},{"when":"Year 5","label":"Curve inverts, recession"},{"when":"Year 6","label":"Rate cuts, new recovery"}],"caption":"The fictional six-year cycle we will follow through this masterclass."},
{"t":"h","text":"The economy in one equation"},
{"t":"p","text":"An economy is the total of everything a country produces and sells. The standard measure is **GDP**, gross domestic product. Economists split it into four kinds of spending, and this split tells you where to look when growth changes."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["GDP","=","Consumers","+","Business invest.","+","Government","+","Net exports"],"caption":"In the US, consumer spending is about two-thirds of the whole economy."},
{"t":"p","text":"Because consumers are such a big piece, anything that changes their income or their confidence moves the whole economy. That is why markets watch jobs, wages, and consumer spending so closely. In many European economies, exports and business investment play a bigger role, so trade news moves markets there more."},
{"t":"h","text":"How the economy reaches a stock price"},
{"t":"p","text":"A stock index price can be split into two parts. The first is **earnings**: how much profit the companies make per share. The second is the **multiple**: how many dollars investors are willing to pay for each dollar of those earnings, the price-to-earnings ratio or P/E."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Price","=","Earnings per share","×","P/E multiple"],"caption":"The economy moves stocks through both parts at once."},
{"t":"p","text":"The economy pushes on each part through a different channel. **Growth** drives earnings: more spending means more sales and profits. **Interest rates** drive the multiple: when safe bonds pay more, investors pay less for risky future profits. **Inflation** sits in the middle, because it pushes interest rates and also squeezes or lifts profits. And over everything floats **risk appetite**: how brave or fearful investors feel."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"grid","items":[{"icon":"📈","label":"Growth","note":"Moves earnings"},{"icon":"🏦","label":"Interest rates","note":"Move the multiple"},{"icon":"🔥","label":"Inflation","note":"Pushes rates, margins"},{"icon":"😨","label":"Risk appetite","note":"Fear vs greed"}],"caption":"Four channels: almost every market headline travels through one of them."},
{"t":"key","text":"Markets do not react to news. They react to the difference between the news and what was already expected."},
{"t":"p","text":"This is the most important idea in the whole masterclass. Prices are set by millions of investors who already know the forecasts. If economists expect 2% growth and growth comes in at 2%, nothing new has happened, and prices barely move. If growth comes in at 4%, that is a **surprise**, and prices adjust. Markets are a machine for pricing the future, so only new information about the future moves them."},
{"t":"example","title":"Daniel’s puzzle, solved in one line","text":"Headline: “Stocks fall as strong jobs data lifts yields.”\nExpected: 150,000 new jobs. Actual: 300,000. Big upside surprise.\nChannel: a hot job market can push up wages and inflation, so investors expect the central bank to keep rates higher for longer.\nResult: bond yields rise, the stock multiple falls, and stocks drop, even though the economy is strong.\nGood news for workers can be bad news for the multiple."},
{"t":"check","q":"A company reports profits up 20%, but the stock falls 5%. Using this chapter, give the most likely reason.","a":"Investors expected more than 20%, or the company’s outlook for the future disappointed. The result was below what was already priced in, so the surprise was negative."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Central banks and the price of money","minutes":9},
{"t":"p","text":"If the economy is a car, the central bank controls the brake and the accelerator. In the US, that is the **Federal Reserve**, or the Fed. Its main tool is the price of overnight money between banks: the **federal funds rate**. Every other interest rate in the economy is influenced by it."},
{"t":"term","word":"Dual mandate","def":"The Fed’s two legal goals: maximum employment and stable prices. It defines stable prices as inflation of about 2% a year over time."},
{"t":"p","text":"The Fed’s rate committee meets about eight times a year. When inflation is too high, it **raises** rates to cool the economy. When unemployment rises and inflation is low, it **cuts** rates to support growth. In Europe, the European Central Bank has one main goal, price stability, so it watches inflation even more closely than jobs."},
{"t":"h","text":"How a rate hike travels through the economy"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"flow","steps":[{"label":"Fed hikes","icon":"🏦","note":"Overnight rate up"},{"label":"Loans cost more","icon":"💳","note":"Mortgages, cards, credit"},{"label":"Spending slows","icon":"🛒","note":"Fewer homes, less hiring"},{"label":"Inflation cools","icon":"🌡️","note":"After a long lag"}],"caption":"The transmission chain. The full effect often takes a year or more."},
{"t":"p","text":"The lag is the tricky part. A rate hike affects new mortgages almost immediately, but the effect on hiring and prices takes many months. So the Fed has to drive by looking at a rear-view mirror, and it often overshoots in one direction or the other. Markets spend a huge amount of energy guessing what the Fed will do next."},
{"t":"example","title":"What a rate change does to a family","text":"A $400,000 30-year fixed mortgage:\nAt 4%: monthly payment about $1,910. Total paid over 30 years: about $687,000.\nAt 7%: monthly payment about $2,661. Total paid: about $958,000.\nThe same house costs $751 more per month, 39% more. Many buyers cannot afford that, so home sales, furniture sales, and construction jobs all slow down. That is the brake working."},
{"t":"h","text":"Why rates move every asset price"},
{"t":"p","text":"Every investment is a claim on future cash. To compare future cash with money today, investors **discount** it: they ask how much money today, invested at the safe interest rate, would grow into that future amount. When the safe rate rises, future cash is worth less today. This one idea explains why rising rates hurt bonds, stocks, and real estate at the same time."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["$100 received in","Value today at 3%","Value today at 6%","Change"],"rows":[["10 years","$74.41","$55.84","−25%"],["30 years","$41.20","$17.41","−58%"]],"caption":"Higher rates shrink the value of distant cash much more than near cash."},
{"t":"p","text":"Look at the last column. Money far in the future is hit much harder. This is why **growth stocks**, whose profits are expected mostly in the distant future, usually fall more when rates rise than **value stocks**, which earn solid profits today. Investors call distant-cash assets “long duration”, borrowing the word from bonds."},
{"t":"h","text":"The bond seesaw, revisited"},
{"t":"p","text":"In 201 you learned that bond prices and yields move in opposite directions. Here are concrete numbers. Imagine bonds that pay a 4% coupon, priced at $100 when market yields are 4%. Now market yields jump to 5%. Older bonds paying only 4% must fall in price until their total return matches the new 5%."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"2-year bond","value":-1.9},{"label":"10-year bond","value":-7.7,"highlight":true},{"label":"30-year bond","value":-15.4}],"unit":"%","caption":"Price change when yields rise from 4% to 5% on a 4% coupon bond."},
{"t":"p","text":"The 2-year bond falls to about $98.14, the 10-year to about $92.28, the 30-year to about $84.63. The longer the bond, the bigger the fall. The handy shortcut is **duration**: a bond fund with a duration of 6 loses about 6% in price for each 1 percentage point rise in yields, and gains about 6% for each 1 point fall."},
{"t":"term","word":"Real interest rate","def":"The interest rate minus inflation. A 5% rate with 3% inflation is a real rate of about 2%. Real rates are what truly drive borrowing, saving and asset prices."},
{"t":"p","text":"Central banks also have a second tool for big emergencies. **Quantitative easing** (QE) means the central bank buys large amounts of bonds to push long-term rates down. **Quantitative tightening** (QT) is the reverse: letting those bonds roll off to let rates rise. You do not need the mechanics; just remember that QE tends to support asset prices and QT tends to remove that support."},
{"t":"example","title":"The cycle, Year 4: the Fed slams the brake","text":"In our fictional cycle, inflation climbs to 6% in Year 4. The Fed raises its rate from 1% to 5% within about a year.\nDaniel’s bond fund (duration 6): 10-year yields rise from 2.5% to 4.5%, so the price falls about 6 × 2 = 12%. Interest income of about 3% softens it to roughly −9% for the year.\nDaniel’s stock funds: earnings are still growing, but the P/E falls from 22 to 17. Even with earnings up 5%, the index changes by 1.05 × (17 ÷ 22) − 1 = about −19%.\nBoth halves of his 60/40 fall in the same year. Chapter 7 explains why this happens."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Run the rate math yourself","prompt":"Daniel holds $160,000 in a bond fund with duration 7 and a yield of 4%. Answer three questions.","steps":["If yields rise by 1.5 percentage points over a year, estimate the price change and the total return including about 4% of income.","If yields instead fall by 1 point, estimate the total return.","Why might a long-term investor actually welcome higher yields?"],"answer":"1. Price change ≈ −7 × 1.5% = −10.5%. Total return ≈ −10.5% + 4% = −6.5%, or about −$10,400 on $160,000.\n2. Price change ≈ −7 × (−1%) = +7%. Total return ≈ 7% + 4% = +11%, or about +$17,600.\n3. After the fall, new money and reinvested coupons earn the higher yield. Over a period longer than the fund’s duration, higher yields mean higher future income, and the investor usually ends up with more money than if yields had stayed low. The pain comes first, the reward comes later."},
{"t":"check","q":"Two stocks earn the same total profits over 20 years. Stock A earns most of them in the first 5 years; Stock B earns most of them in the last 5. Which one falls more when rates rise?","a":"Stock B. Its cash arrives much later, and distant cash loses more present value when the discount rate rises. It behaves like a long-duration bond."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Inflation: the quiet tax","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Inflation is the rise in the general level of prices. A little of it, around 2%, is considered healthy: it encourages spending and investment, and gives the central bank room to cut rates in a downturn. Too much inflation destroys savings, confuses planning, and forces the central bank to slam the brake."},
{"t":"h","text":"How inflation is measured"},
{"t":"p","text":"The headline number in the US is the **Consumer Price Index**, or CPI. Government statisticians track the price of a large basket of goods and services that a typical household buys: rent, food, gasoline, medical care, clothes, haircuts. The CPI compares the cost of that basket over time."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"donut","data":[{"label":"Housing","value":35},{"label":"Transport","value":16},{"label":"Food","value":14},{"label":"Medical","value":8},{"label":"Energy","value":7},{"label":"Everything else","value":20}],"center":"CPI basket","caption":"An illustrative CPI basket: housing is by far the biggest piece."},
{"t":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**Headline CPI** includes everything, so it jumps around with gasoline and food prices.","**Core CPI** removes food and energy to show the underlying trend. Markets often care more about core.","**PCE** is a similar index from a different agency. It is the measure the Fed prefers for its 2% target.","**Services inflation** (rent, insurance, wages-heavy services) tends to be sticky, so it is watched closely."]},
{"t":"h","text":"Monthly vs yearly numbers"},
{"t":"p","text":"CPI is reported both as a change from last month and a change from twelve months ago. Professionals often look at the monthly number first, because it shows the most recent trend. A monthly change of 0.3% sounds tiny, but if it repeated for a year it would compound to about 3.7%, almost double the Fed’s target."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"stat","items":[{"value":"0.2%","label":"a month ≈ 2.4% a year"},{"value":"0.3%","label":"a month ≈ 3.7% a year"},{"value":"0.4%","label":"a month ≈ 4.9% a year"}],"caption":"Annualizing monthly inflation: (1 + monthly)¹² − 1."},
{"t":"warn","text":"Watch out for **base effects**. If prices jumped a lot twelve months ago, the yearly number will fall sharply when that big month drops out of the calculation, even if prices are still rising fast today. Always check the monthly trend too."},
{"t":"h","text":"Who wins and who loses"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Hurt by inflation","icon":"📉","items":["Cash savers","Fixed-rate bond holders","Workers whose pay lags","High-P/E growth stocks"]},"right":{"title":"Can benefit","icon":"📈","items":["Fixed-rate borrowers","Firms with pricing power","Commodity producers","Inflation-linked bonds"]},"caption":"Unexpected inflation moves wealth from lenders to borrowers."},
{"t":"p","text":"Think about a fixed-rate mortgage. If you borrowed at 3% and inflation runs at 6%, you repay the loan with dollars that are losing value faster than the interest you pay. The bank loses, you win. A bond is the same contract seen from the lender’s side, which is why bonds hate surprise inflation."},
{"t":"p","text":"Stocks are more mixed. Companies own real assets and can raise prices, so over long periods stocks have usually outpaced inflation. But in the short run, high and rising inflation hurts stocks, because it pushes rates up, which lowers the multiple, and it squeezes the profit margins of firms that cannot pass on higher costs."},
{"t":"term","word":"TIPS","def":"Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities: US government bonds whose principal rises with CPI. They protect against inflation surprises, though their price still moves with real interest rates."},
{"t":"h","text":"Real returns: the only returns that matter"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["Real return","≈","Nominal return","−","Inflation"],"caption":"The exact version: (1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Calculate real returns","prompt":"Daniel compares three places for money over one year. Find the real return of each using the exact formula.","steps":["A savings account paying 4% when inflation is 3%.","A bond fund returning 4% when inflation is 6%.","A stock fund returning 9% when inflation is 3%."],"answer":"Exact formula: (1 + nominal) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1.\n1. Savings: 1.04 ÷ 1.03 − 1 = 0.97%. About +1% of real buying power.\n2. Bond fund: 1.04 ÷ 1.06 − 1 = −1.89%. He ends with more dollars but can buy less. A real loss of almost 2%.\n3. Stocks: 1.09 ÷ 1.03 − 1 = 5.83%. The shortcut 9% − 3% = 6% is close; the gap grows when inflation is high.\nLesson: a positive nominal return can still be a real loss. Always ask what the return is after inflation."},
{"t":"check","q":"Core CPI rose 0.5% this month, but yearly CPI fell from 4% to 3.6%. Is inflation pressure getting better or worse?","a":"Probably worse right now. A 0.5% monthly rise annualizes to about 6.2%. The yearly figure fell mainly because a big month from a year ago dropped out. That is a base effect, not real progress."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"The business cycle","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Economies do not grow in a straight line. They breathe in and out: periods of expansion are followed by slowdowns and, every several years, a recession. This rhythm is the **business cycle**. It is irregular, not a clock, but it has recognizable phases."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"cycle","steps":["Early recovery","Mid expansion","Late cycle","Recession"],"center":"Business cycle","caption":"Four phases, of very different lengths: expansions usually last years, recessions months."},
{"t":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**Early recovery:** growth restarts from a low point, rates are low, profits rebound fast, unemployment is still high but starting to fall.","**Mid expansion:** the longest phase. Steady growth, healthy hiring, moderate inflation, the central bank slowly normalizes rates.","**Late cycle:** the economy runs hot. Unemployment is very low, wages and inflation rise, the central bank hikes, credit gets tighter.","**Recession:** spending and output fall, layoffs rise, profits drop, inflation eases, the central bank cuts rates."]},
{"t":"p","text":"In the US, recessions are officially dated by a committee of economists at the National Bureau of Economic Research, often many months after they start. The popular rule of “two negative quarters of GDP” is a rough shortcut, not the official definition. Since the Second World War, US expansions have lasted on average about five years, and recessions on average less than a year."},
{"t":"h","text":"Leading, coincident and lagging signals"},
{"t":"p","text":"Because the official verdict comes late, investors watch indicators that tend to turn _before_ the economy does. Knowing which kind of indicator you are reading prevents a lot of confusion."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Type","Turns","Examples"],"rows":[["Leading","Before the economy","Yield curve, PMI orders"],["Leading","Before the economy","Jobless claims, permits"],["Coincident","With the economy","Payrolls, industry output"],["Lagging","After the economy","Unemployment rate"],["Lagging","After the economy","Core services inflation"]],"caption":"Stocks themselves are a leading indicator: they usually turn before the economy."},
{"t":"p","text":"Here is a surprising consequence. The **unemployment rate** is a lagging indicator: it usually peaks after the recession has ended. So by the time unemployment looks terrible, the stock market has often already started rising. Investors who wait for the jobs data to improve before buying usually arrive late."},
{"t":"term","word":"PMI","def":"Purchasing Managers’ Index: a monthly survey of company buyers. Above 50 means activity is expanding, below 50 means it is shrinking. New orders inside the PMI are an early signal."},
{"t":"example","title":"The cycle, Years 1 to 3","text":"Year 1, early recovery: GDP growth turns positive at 3%, unemployment is 7% but falling, the Fed rate is near 0%. Stocks rise 25% as earnings rebound from a low base.\nYear 2, mid expansion: growth 2.5%, unemployment 5%, inflation 2%. Stocks rise 12%, driven by earnings.\nYear 3, still mid expansion: growth 2.5%, unemployment 4%, inflation 2.5%. The Fed begins small hikes to 1%. Stocks rise 9%.\nDaniel’s 60/40 does very well, and his biggest risk is starting to feel that this will last forever."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Name the phase","prompt":"For each fictional data snapshot, name the most likely phase of the cycle and say which single clue gave it away.","steps":["A: PMI 47 and falling, jobless claims up 30% in three months, central bank has just made its first cut, unemployment 4.3% and rising.","B: GDP growth 4%, unemployment 3.5%, wages up 6%, core inflation 4.5%, central bank hiking at every meeting.","C: Unemployment 8% but stopped rising, PMI back above 50 from 42, rates near zero, stocks up 30% from their low."],"answer":"A: Recession, or entering one. Key clue: jobless claims rising fast and a falling PMI below 50 are leading signals of a contraction; the first rate cut confirms the central bank sees it too. Unemployment at 4.3% looks fine but it is lagging.\nB: Late cycle. Key clue: wages and core inflation running well above the 2% target with very low unemployment: the economy is overheating and the central bank is braking.\nC: Early recovery. Key clue: the PMI crossing back above 50 while unemployment is still high. The stock market has already moved first, as usual."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"The yield curve: the market’s forecast","minutes":7},
{"t":"p","text":"The **yield curve** is a simple chart: the interest rates on government bonds of different maturities, from three months to thirty years, connected by a line. It is one of the most famous recession signals in finance, and you can read it in ten seconds."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"line","series":[{"name":"Normal","points":[2.0,2.6,3.1,3.6,4.0]},{"name":"Flat","points":[4.3,4.3,4.3,4.3,4.4]},{"name":"Inverted","points":[5.3,4.8,4.3,4.0,4.2]}],"xLabels":["3 mo","2 yr","5 yr","10 yr","30 yr"],"unit":"%","caption":"Three illustrative shapes of the Treasury yield curve."},
{"t":"h","text":"Why it normally slopes up"},
{"t":"p","text":"Normally, lending money for longer pays more. If you lock your money in for ten years, you want extra compensation for the risk that inflation or rates change. So a healthy economy usually has an upward curve: short rates lower, long rates higher."},
{"t":"p","text":"Short-term rates are mostly set by the central bank today. Long-term rates are set by the market and reflect what investors expect short rates, growth and inflation to be over the next decade. So the curve is really a comparison: **the central bank’s policy now vs the market’s view of the future.**"},
{"t":"h","text":"What an inversion means"},
{"t":"p","text":"When short rates rise above long rates, the curve is **inverted**. The message from the market is: the central bank has made money so tight that it will slow the economy, and rates will have to come down later. In the US, an inverted curve has come before most recessions of the last several decades, usually with a delay of about six months to two years. It has also given false or very early signals, so treat it as a warning light, not a calendar."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"equation","parts":["2s10s spread","=","10-year yield","−","2-year yield"],"caption":"The most quoted measure of the curve’s slope. Below zero means inverted."},
{"t":"warn","text":"The recession often arrives not during the inversion, but when the curve starts to **steepen again** because the central bank is cutting short rates in a hurry. Do not relax just because an inversion has ended."},
{"t":"h","text":"Credit spreads: the fear gauge of lenders"},
{"t":"p","text":"A second market signal lives in corporate bonds. Companies pay more than the government to borrow, because they can default. The extra yield is the **credit spread**. When investors feel safe, spreads are narrow. When they fear defaults, spreads widen fast, often before stocks fully react."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"stack","columns":[{"title":"Calm market","blocks":[{"label":"Treasury 4%","value":4},{"label":"Spread 1.5%","value":1.5}]},{"title":"Stressed market","blocks":[{"label":"Treasury 3.5%","value":3.5},{"label":"Spread 4%","value":4}]}],"unit":"%","caption":"A company’s borrowing rate = safe rate + credit spread. In stress, the spread can more than double."},
{"t":"p","text":"Notice something in the stressed market: the Treasury yield fell, because scared investors bought safe government bonds, yet the company pays more in total, 7.5% instead of 5.5%. That is why high-quality government bonds can rise in a recession while lower-quality corporate bonds fall."},
{"t":"example","title":"The cycle, Years 4 and 5: the curve speaks","text":"End of Year 3: 2-year yield 1.5%, 10-year 2.5%. Spread +1.0%: normal.\nMid Year 4: the Fed hikes to 5%. 2-year 4.9%, 10-year 4.5%. Spread −0.4%: inverted. Stocks are already down 19%, as we saw.\nYear 5: the economy slows, layoffs begin, a recession starts in the second half. The Fed cuts to 3%. 2-year 3.0%, 10-year 3.9%. Spread +0.9%: the curve has steepened again.\nCredit spreads on lower-quality bonds widen from 3.5% to 7%. Daniel reads “curve no longer inverted, but recession confirmed” and finally understands the warning above."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Read the curve","prompt":"Calculate the 2s10s spread for each fictional moment and write one sentence on what the market is saying.","steps":["Moment 1: 2-year 1.0%, 10-year 2.8%.","Moment 2: 2-year 4.6%, 10-year 4.1%.","Moment 3: 2-year 2.9%, 10-year 3.7%, and the central bank cut rates twice in three months."],"answer":"1. 2.8% − 1.0% = +1.8%. Steep, normal curve: policy is easy and the market expects growth and rising rates ahead. Typical of early recovery.\n2. 4.1% − 4.6% = −0.5%. Inverted: policy is tight and the market expects the central bank to cut later because growth will weaken. A late-cycle warning light.\n3. 3.7% − 2.9% = +0.8%. The curve is steepening again, but because short rates are falling fast. Combined with emergency cuts, this is the pattern that often appears as a recession arrives."},
{"t":"check","q":"If the 10-year yield falls from 4% to 3.5% while the 2-year stays at 4.5%, did the curve become more or less inverted?","a":"More inverted. The spread moves from −0.5% to −1.0%. The market is pricing more future weakness and future rate cuts."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"The earnings cycle","minutes":6},
{"t":"p","text":"So far we have looked at the economy from above. Now we zoom into companies, because in the long run stock prices follow **earnings**. Over decades, the growth of corporate profits explains most of the growth in stock prices. Over months, the multiple dominates. Over a full cycle, both matter."},
{"t":"h","text":"Why profits swing more than the economy"},
{"t":"p","text":"When GDP falls 2% in a recession, corporate profits often fall 15% or 20%. Why so much more? Because of **operating leverage**: many company costs are fixed. Rent, salaries, machines and software cost about the same whether sales are high or low. So a small change in sales becomes a big change in profit."},
{"t":"example","title":"Operating leverage in one table","text":"A company with $1,000 of sales, $600 of fixed costs, and variable costs of 20% of sales.\nNormal year: $1,000 − $600 − $200 = $200 of profit.\nSales fall 10% to $900: $900 − $600 − $180 = $120. Profit falls **40%**.\nSales rise 10% to $1,100: $1,100 − $600 − $220 = $280. Profit rises **40%**.\nA 10% move in sales became a 40% move in profit. That is why stocks are so sensitive to the cycle."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"bars","data":[{"label":"Sales −10%","value":-40},{"label":"Normal","value":0},{"label":"Sales +10%","value":40,"highlight":true}],"unit":"%","caption":"Change in profit for the example company: fixed costs amplify every move in sales."},
{"t":"p","text":"Operating leverage is different across industries. Airlines, carmakers, chemical producers and chipmakers have high fixed costs and very cyclical demand, so their profits swing wildly. Food, household products, utilities and healthcare sell things people buy in every economy, so their profits are steadier. Investors call the first group **cyclicals** and the second **defensives**."},
{"t":"h","text":"Earnings season and the expectations game"},
{"t":"p","text":"Public companies in the US report results every quarter. Most of them report in a busy few weeks after each quarter ends, called **earnings season**. Before each report, analysts publish forecasts, and the average forecast is the **consensus**. As you learned in chapter 1, the stock reacts to the gap between results and consensus, and even more to **guidance**, the company’s own outlook for the next quarters."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"matrix","xAxis":"Guidance →","yAxis":"Results vs consensus →","cells":[{"title":"Mixed, often down","note":"Beat, but weak outlook"},{"title":"Strong up","note":"Beat and raise"},{"title":"Strong down","note":"Miss and cut"},{"title":"Mixed, often up","note":"Miss, but strong outlook"}],"caption":"Guidance often matters more than the quarter just reported, because prices look forward."},
{"t":"p","text":"There is also a ritual worth knowing. Companies often guide analysts toward numbers they can beat. As a result, in a typical quarter a large majority of companies report earnings above consensus. A “beat” is therefore normal, not special. What tells you something is the size of the beat, the trend in guidance, and whether analysts raise or cut their forecasts for next year."},
{"t":"h","text":"How the cycle shows up in index earnings"},
{"t":"p","text":"Let us put numbers on our fictional index. At the end of Year 3, earnings per share are $200 and the P/E is 22, so the index stands at 200 × 22 = 4,400."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Moment","EPS","P/E","Index"],"rows":[["End Year 3","$200","22","4,400"],["End Year 4","$210","17","3,570"],["End Year 5","$178.50","16","2,856"],["End Year 6","$185.64","18.8","3,490"]],"caption":"Year 4 falls on the multiple, Year 5 on earnings, Year 6 rises on the multiple first."},
{"t":"p","text":"Read the story in the table. In Year 4 earnings still grow 5%, but the index falls about 19% because rates crush the multiple. In Year 5 the recession hits profits: EPS falls 15%, and the index falls another 20%. In Year 6, earnings are still weak, up only 4%, yet the index rises 22%. Why? Because the market has started to price the recovery and the falling rates **before** profits actually recover."},
{"t":"key","text":"Stocks usually bottom while the news and the earnings are still bad, because prices look six to twelve months ahead."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Decompose an index move","prompt":"A fictional index stands at 4,000 with EPS of $200, so a P/E of 20. Over the next year, EPS falls 10% and the P/E rises to 21 because investors expect rate cuts. Find the new index level and split the change into an earnings part and a multiple part.","steps":["New EPS = $200 × 0.90.","New index = new EPS × new P/E.","Earnings effect alone: new EPS × old P/E.","Multiple effect: the rest."],"answer":"New EPS = $180. New index = 180 × 21 = 3,780. Total change: 3,780 ÷ 4,000 − 1 = −5.5%.\nEarnings effect alone: 180 × 20 = 3,600, which is −10%.\nMultiple effect: from 3,600 to 3,780 is +5% (21 ÷ 20 = 1.05).\nCombined: 0.90 × 1.05 = 0.945, so −5.5%. The falling profits were partly offset by investors paying more for each dollar of future profit, a typical pattern near the end of a downturn."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"How each asset class behaves in each phase","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Now we can bring everything together. Each asset class responds to the four channels in its own way. The most useful map uses just two questions: **is growth rising or falling?** and **is inflation rising or falling?** That gives four economic seasons."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"matrix","xAxis":"Inflation rising →","yAxis":"Growth rising →","cells":[{"title":"Goldilocks","note":"Stocks shine, bonds fine"},{"title":"Overheating","note":"Commodities, value, TIPS"},{"title":"Deflationary bust","note":"Govt bonds, cash"},{"title":"Stagflation","note":"Cash, commodities, TIPS"}],"caption":"Four seasons of the economy and the assets that tend to suit each one."},
{"t":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**Goldilocks** (growth up, inflation down): the best season for stocks, especially growth stocks. Bonds are steady. Most of a long expansion feels like this.","**Overheating** (growth up, inflation up): commodities and energy do well, value and cyclical stocks beat growth stocks, bonds suffer as rates rise.","**Stagflation** (growth down, inflation up): the hardest season. Stocks and bonds both struggle. Cash, short-term bonds, TIPS and commodities hold up best.","**Deflationary bust** (growth down, inflation down): a classic recession. High-quality government bonds rally as rates are cut; stocks and corporate credit fall."]},
{"t":"h","text":"The cycle, phase by phase"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Phase","Stocks","Bonds","Cash"],"rows":[["Early recovery","Strong, cyclicals lead","OK","Weak"],["Mid expansion","Good, steady","Modest","Modest"],["Late cycle","Volatile, value, energy","Weak as rates rise","Improving"],["Recession","Falls, then bottoms","Govt bonds strong","Safe, yields fall"]],"caption":"Typical tendencies, not guarantees. Every cycle has its own personality."},
{"t":"p","text":"Real estate behaves like a mix: rents tend to rise with inflation, but property values are very sensitive to mortgage and lending rates, so real estate often struggles late in the cycle. Gold has no earnings and no coupon; it tends to do best when real interest rates fall or fear spikes. Crypto, which you met in 101 as speculative, has mostly behaved like a high-risk asset that falls hard when money gets tighter."},
{"t":"h","text":"Sector rotation, in brief"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"spectrum","left":"Defensive","right":"Cyclical","items":[{"label":"Utilities","pos":8},{"label":"Staples","pos":18},{"label":"Healthcare","pos":30},{"label":"Tech","pos":60},{"label":"Financials","pos":72},{"label":"Industrials","pos":82},{"label":"Energy, materials","pos":92}],"caption":"Cyclical sectors tend to lead in recoveries; defensives tend to hold up in slowdowns."},
{"t":"p","text":"You will often read that investors are “rotating into defensives” or “buying cyclicals on recovery hopes”. Now you can decode it: they are betting on a change of season. Remember that professionals who try to time these rotations often get them wrong, because they need to be right twice: when to get in and when to get out."},
{"t":"h","text":"Why stocks and bonds sometimes fall together"},
{"t":"p","text":"In most recessions, stocks fall and government bonds rise, so a 60/40 portfolio is cushioned. That is the deflationary bust season. But when the problem is **inflation**, the central bank raises rates, and higher rates hurt both bonds and stocks at the same time. That is exactly what happened in Year 4 of our cycle. The correlation between stocks and bonds is not fixed: it depends on whether growth or inflation is the main worry."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"line","series":[{"name":"60/40 portfolio","points":[100,116.6,126.4,133.7,113.7,103.5,119.6]},{"name":"100% stocks","points":[100,125,140,152.6,123.6,98.9,120.6]}],"xLabels":["Start","Y1","Y2","Y3","Y4","Y5","Y6"],"unit":"","caption":"Our fictional cycle: the 60/40 mix falls far less and ends almost level with all stocks."},
{"t":"p","text":"Here is the yearly math for Daniel’s 60/40. Year 4: 0.6 × (−19%) + 0.4 × (−9%) = −15%. Both halves fell. Year 5: 0.6 × (−20%) + 0.4 × (+7.6%) = −8.96%. Now bonds did their job: the 10-year yield fell about 0.6 points, so his duration-6 bond fund gained about 3.6% in price plus 4% in income. Year 6: 0.6 × 22% + 0.4 × 6% = +15.6%."},
{"t":"p","text":"In dollars: had Daniel entered Year 4 with $400,000, he would have seen $340,000 at the end of Year 4, about $309,500 at the end of Year 5, and about $357,800 at the end of Year 6. An all-stock investor starting at the same $400,000 would have hit a low near $259,000. Daniel’s worst drop was about 23%; the all-stock investor’s was about 35%. And over the full six years, starting from the same amount, the two portfolios ended within about one percentage point of each other, as the chart shows."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Stress-test Daniel’s portfolio","prompt":"Daniel has $400,000: 60% stocks and 40% bonds with a duration of 6. Estimate the one-year result in two scenarios and say which season each one is.","steps":["Scenario A: stocks −25%. Inflation falls fast, yields fall 1.5 points, bonds earn 4% of income on top of the price change.","Scenario B: stocks −15%. Inflation surges, yields rise 2 points, bonds earn 4% of income.","For each: bond return = −duration × yield change + income. Then combine 60/40."],"answer":"Scenario A (deflationary bust): bond return = −6 × (−1.5%) + 4% = 9% + 4% = +13%. Portfolio = 0.6 × (−25%) + 0.4 × 13% = −15% + 5.2% = −9.8%, or about −$39,200. Bonds cushioned the fall a lot.\nScenario B (stagflation): bond return = −6 × 2% + 4% = −8%. Portfolio = 0.6 × (−15%) + 0.4 × (−8%) = −9% − 3.2% = −12.2%, or about −$48,800. A smaller stock fall, but a worse total, because bonds failed as a hedge.\nTakeaway: bonds protect best against growth shocks, less against inflation shocks. Some investors add a slice of TIPS, short-term bonds or cash to cover the inflation season. Diversifying across seasons is more robust than predicting them."},
{"t":"warn","text":"This map is for understanding, not for trading. The seasons are only clear in hindsight. A long-term investor uses it to build a portfolio that can survive all four, then rebalances calmly when one season hurts."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Reading economic data releases","minutes":7},
{"t":"p","text":"The economy speaks through a calendar of official data releases. Most of them come out at 8:30 in the morning, New York time, before the stock market opens. That is why the morning is when markets often move most sharply on macro news, and why the app’s morning news report is so useful for you."},
{"t":"h","text":"The big four and the supporting cast"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Release","When","What to watch"],"rows":[["Jobs report","Monthly, often 1st Friday","Payrolls, wages, unemployment"],["CPI","Monthly, mid-month","Core monthly change"],["Fed decision","About 8 times a year","Rate, statement, projections"],["GDP","Quarterly, revised twice","Consumer spending, growth rate"],["Jobless claims","Weekly, Thursday","Trend of new claims"],["PCE, retail, PMI","Monthly","Fed’s inflation gauge, demand"]],"caption":"The core US data calendar. Exact dates change, so check the calendar each week."},
{"t":"h","text":"The jobs report, decoded"},
{"t":"p","text":"The monthly employment report has two surveys inside it. One asks businesses how many people they pay: that gives **nonfarm payrolls**, the number of jobs added. The other asks households if they are working: that gives the **unemployment rate**. Markets also look closely at **average hourly earnings**, because fast wage growth can feed inflation."},
{"t":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**Revisions:** the previous two months are revised. A strong headline with big downward revisions can be weaker than it looks.","**Breakeven pace:** the economy needs a certain number of new jobs each month just to keep unemployment steady as the population grows. Beating that pace means a tightening labor market.","**Wages:** monthly wage growth of 0.3% is roughly consistent with a healthy, non-inflationary economy; 0.5% or more starts to worry the central bank."]},
{"t":"h","text":"GDP, decoded"},
{"t":"p","text":"US GDP is reported as an **annualized** growth rate: the quarterly change multiplied up as if it lasted a full year. A quarter where the economy grows 0.5% is reported as about 2.0% (1.005⁴ − 1 = 2.02%). In Europe and Italy, GDP is usually reported as the plain quarterly change, so a “0.5%” headline in Milan and a “2.0%” headline in New York can describe the same speed. Always check which convention you are reading."},
{"t":"p","text":"GDP is also old news by the time it is published: it describes a quarter that has already ended, and it is revised twice. Markets usually react more to the jobs report, CPI and the Fed, which are fresher and more surprising."},
{"t":"h","text":"The surprise is the signal"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"flow","steps":[{"label":"Consensus","icon":"📋","note":"What was expected"},{"label":"Actual","icon":"📰","note":"What came out"},{"label":"Surprise","icon":"⚡","note":"Actual − expected"},{"label":"Channel","icon":"🔀","note":"Growth, rates, inflation"},{"label":"Reaction","icon":"📊","note":"Yields, stocks, dollar"}],"caption":"The five-step reading of any data release."},
{"t":"p","text":"Every economic calendar shows three columns: the **previous** value, the **consensus** forecast, and the **actual** number once released. Your job is to compare actual to consensus, ask which channel the surprise travels through, and then predict the direction of yields, stocks and the dollar. With practice, it takes a few seconds."},
{"t":"p","text":"The context also matters. When inflation is the main worry, markets read every strong number through the rates channel: strong data means higher yields and often lower stocks. When recession is the main worry, markets read data through the growth channel: strong data is a relief and stocks rise. The same number can produce opposite reactions in different seasons. Investors call this the **regime** of the market."},
{"t":"example","title":"The same surprise, two regimes","text":"Payrolls: consensus 150,000, actual 280,000.\nRegime 1, Year 4 of our cycle, inflation at 6%: markets fear more rate hikes. 2-year yield +0.15%, stocks −1.5%, dollar up.\nRegime 2, Year 5, recession fears dominate: markets feel relief that the job market is holding. Stocks +1.2%, yields up a little, credit spreads narrow.\nSame data, opposite stock reaction. The regime decides which channel wins."},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Call the reaction","prompt":"For each fictional release, compute the surprise, name the channel, and predict the first move in 2-year yields and in stocks. Assume a late-cycle regime where the central bank is fighting inflation.","steps":["Core CPI month-on-month: consensus 0.3%, actual 0.5%.","Payrolls: consensus 180,000, actual 60,000, unemployment up from 4.0% to 4.3%.","Retail sales: consensus +0.4%, actual +0.4%, previous month revised from +0.6% to +0.2%."],"answer":"1. Surprise +0.2 points on core CPI, a big miss on the hot side (0.5% a month annualizes to about 6.2%). Channel: inflation → rates. 2-year yields up sharply, stocks down, growth and long-duration stocks hit hardest.\n2. Surprise −120,000 jobs and a jump in unemployment. Channel: growth, but in an inflation-fighting regime it also means the central bank can stop hiking. First move: 2-year yields fall clearly. Stocks may rise at first on hopes of rate cuts, but if the data suggests recession, the reaction can turn negative within days.\n3. No surprise on the headline, but the downward revision of the previous month is a hidden negative: demand was weaker than thought. Small move: yields slightly down, stocks roughly flat. Lesson: always read the revisions."},
{"t":"check","q":"US GDP is reported at 3.0% annualized. Roughly what was the actual growth during the quarter?","a":"About 0.74%, because 1.0074⁴ ≈ 1.030. A useful shortcut is to divide the annualized rate by 4."},
{"t":"chapter","title":"Your morning market news framework","minutes":8},
{"t":"p","text":"Daniel now understands the machine. The last step is turning that knowledge into a quick, calm routine for the morning news, one that makes him smarter without making him trade. Here is a five-step framework that fits between two subway stations."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"pyramid","levels":["My plan: any action?","Why: which channel","Surprise vs expected","What moved","What is the season"],"caption":"Read from the base up. Most mornings, the top level is: no action needed."},
{"t":"h","text":"Step 1. What is the season?"},
{"t":"p","text":"Before reading any headline, remind yourself of the backdrop in one sentence. For example: “Late cycle, inflation still above target, central bank on hold, curve inverted.” Update it only when something big changes. This sentence is your regime: it tells you which channel will dominate how markets read the news."},
{"t":"h","text":"Step 2. What moved, and how much?"},
{"t":"p","text":"Look at a small dashboard, always the same one, so your eye learns what is normal. Five numbers are enough. Compare each move to its usual daily size before calling it big."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"table","head":["Indicator","Normal day","Big day"],"rows":[["Stock index","±0.5% to 1%","±2% or more"],["10-year yield","±0.03 to 0.05 pts","±0.15 pts or more"],["2-year yield","±0.03 to 0.05 pts","±0.15 pts or more"],["Dollar index","±0.3%","±1% or more"],["Oil","±1% to 2%","±5% or more"]],"caption":"Illustrative ranges that help you judge whether a move is noise or news."},
{"t":"h","text":"Step 3. What was the surprise?"},
{"t":"p","text":"Find the reason behind the move. Usually it is one of four things: a data release versus consensus, a central bank decision or speech, a big company’s earnings, or a geopolitical or policy shock. If there was no surprise, the move is probably noise: positioning, flows, or simply the random daily breathing of prices."},
{"t":"h","text":"Step 4. Which channel?"},
{"t":"p","text":"Ask which of the four channels from chapter 1 the surprise travels through: growth, rates, inflation or risk appetite. Then check if the market moves are consistent with your reading. A clean inflation scare shows yields up, stocks down, and growth stocks hit hardest. A clean growth scare shows yields down, stocks down, cyclicals hit hardest, and credit spreads wider. A clean relief rally shows stocks up, credit spreads tighter, and calmer volatility."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"compare","left":{"title":"Inflation scare","icon":"🔥","items":["2-yr yield up","Stocks down","Growth stocks worst","Dollar up"]},"right":{"title":"Growth scare","icon":"🧊","items":["Yields down","Stocks down","Cyclicals worst","Credit spreads wider"]},"caption":"Two kinds of bad days. The fingerprints tell you which worry is driving markets."},
{"t":"h","text":"Step 5. So what for my plan?"},
{"t":"p","text":"This is the step most people skip, and the most important. For a long-term investor, the answer is almost always: nothing changes today. The news may affect your understanding, your expectations, or your timing for a rebalance you already planned. It should almost never trigger a trade on its own."},
{"t":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**Valid reasons to act:** your allocation drifted outside its rebalancing band; your own goals, job or horizon changed; you found a cheaper fund that does the same job.","**Not valid reasons to act:** a scary headline; a prediction by a famous investor; a strong opinion about the next Fed meeting; a big move in one day.","**Useful reasons to learn:** connecting a headline to the channels in this masterclass, and checking whether your portfolio is built to survive all four seasons."]},
{"t":"example","title":"Daniel’s morning, worked","text":"Headline: “Futures slide, 2-year yield jumps 0.18 to 4.9% after hot CPI; tech leads losses.”\n1. Season: late cycle, inflation above target, central bank fighting it.\n2. What moved: 2-year yield +0.18 points is a big day; futures −1.4% is a large but not extreme move.\n3. Surprise: core CPI 0.5% vs 0.3% expected.\n4. Channel: inflation → rates. Fingerprints match: short yields up, long-duration tech stocks hit hardest.\n5. My plan: my 60/40 is now about 58/42, inside my 5-point band. No action. I note that rate cuts are now less likely and move on to my book."},
{"t":"fig","kind":"chat","messages":[{"role":"you","text":"Morning report says stocks rallied 2% on a weak jobs number. Why would weak jobs be good?"},{"role":"ai","text":"Check the regime first. If inflation is the main worry, weak hiring lowers the chance of more rate hikes. Yields fall, the multiple rises, stocks rally."},{"role":"you","text":"So it is the rates channel, not the growth channel?"},{"role":"ai","text":"Yes, for today. If weak data keeps coming, markets may switch to the growth channel and treat it as recession news instead."}],"caption":"Using an AI assistant to test your reading. Ask why, not what to buy."},
{"t":"h","text":"Common traps in the morning news"},
{"t":"list","ordered":false,"items":["**The “because” trap.** Journalists must explain every move, even random ones. A 0.3% move rarely has a real cause.","**Point-based headlines.** “Index drops 400 points” sounds dramatic; at a high index level it may be just 1%. Always convert to percent.","**Forecast fever.** Nobody consistently predicts recessions, rate decisions or year-end index levels. Read forecasts as opinions.","**Recency bias.** After three bad days, it feels like the trend will continue forever. Look at your five-year chart instead."]},
{"t":"exercise","title":"Run the framework on three mornings","prompt":"Daniel’s season sentence is: “Late cycle, inflation 3.5% and slowly falling, central bank on hold, curve inverted.” Apply the five steps to each fictional morning headline.","steps":["Morning A: “Stocks edge up 0.3% in quiet trading; yields flat ahead of next week’s Fed meeting.”","Morning B: “Treasury yields plunge, 2-year down 0.25, as payrolls miss badly and unemployment jumps; banks and industrials slide.”","Morning C: “Oil surges 9% after supply disruption; energy stocks rally, airlines fall, 10-year yield up 0.10.”"],"answer":"Morning A: What moved: nothing beyond a normal day. Surprise: none. Channel: none. Plan: no action. This is noise; skip to your book.\nMorning B: What moved: a very big fall in 2-year yields, stocks down with cyclicals worst. Surprise: weak jobs and rising unemployment. Channel: growth scare, the fingerprints match (yields down, cyclicals worst). The regime may be shifting from inflation fear to recession fear. Plan: check the allocation; bonds probably gained and stocks fell, so the mix may have drifted. Rebalance only if outside the band. No forecasting trades.\nMorning C: What moved: oil a big day, sector moves, yields up a bit. Surprise: an energy supply shock. Channel: inflation, with a possible hit to growth if it lasts, a stagflation flavor. Winners and losers follow costs: producers up, fuel users down. Plan: no action. Note that commodities and TIPS are the assets that suit this season, a reason long-term diversification across seasons exists."},
{"t":"h","text":"Bringing it all home"},
{"t":"fig","kind":"stat","items":[{"value":"4","label":"channels: growth, rates, inflation, risk"},{"value":"4","label":"seasons of growth and inflation"},{"value":"5","label":"steps to read any morning"}],"caption":"The whole masterclass in three numbers."},
{"t":"p","text":"Daniel started with one puzzle: why good news can be bad news. He now reads the economy as a machine with four channels, knows that only surprises move prices, follows the central bank through the price of money, watches inflation in monthly steps, reads the cycle through leading signals and the yield curve, understands why earnings swing, and knows which assets suit which season."},
{"t":"p","text":"Most importantly, he has learned what all this knowledge is _for_. It is not for predicting next month. It is for building a portfolio that can survive every season, for staying calm when headlines shout, and for enjoying the morning news as a story he finally understands. That is what it means to be an informed long-term investor."},
{"t":"tip","text":"Tomorrow morning, before reading the market report, write your season sentence in your notes app. Then run the five steps on the first headline you see."}
],
"takeaways":[
"Stock prices equal earnings times a multiple: growth moves earnings, interest rates move the multiple.",
"Markets react to surprises versus expectations, and the regime decides which channel a surprise travels through.",
"Rate hikes cool the economy with a long lag and hit long-duration assets, like long bonds and growth stocks, hardest.",
"An inverted yield curve, wider credit spreads and rising jobless claims are warning lights, not calendars.",
"Read the morning news in five steps: season, moves, surprise, channel, plan; the plan rarely changes."
],
"quiz":[
{"q":"Payrolls come in far above consensus while inflation is the market’s main worry. What is the most likely first reaction?","options":["Yields fall and stocks rise strongly","Yields rise and stocks fall","Nothing, because jobs data does not matter","Gold and long bonds both rally"],"answer":1,"why":"In an inflation-fighting regime, strong jobs data raises the chance of higher rates for longer, which pushes yields up and the stock multiple down."},
{"q":"Why do growth stocks usually fall more than value stocks when interest rates rise?","options":["Growth companies always carry more debt","Value stocks pay no dividends","More of their cash flows are far in the future, so discounting hurts them more","Rates only affect technology companies"],"answer":2,"why":"Distant cash loses more present value when the discount rate rises, so long-duration assets like growth stocks are more rate-sensitive."},
{"q":"A bond fund has a duration of 5. Yields rise by 1 percentage point. Roughly what happens to its price?","options":["It falls about 5%","It rises about 5%","It falls about 1%","It falls about 50%"],"answer":0,"why":"Duration approximates the percent price change for a 1-point change in yields, in the opposite direction."},
{"q":"Monthly core CPI is 0.4%. About what yearly pace does that imply if it continues?","options":["0.4%","2.0%","About 3.0%","About 4.9%"],"answer":3,"why":"(1.004)¹² − 1 ≈ 4.9%, far above a 2% target. Small monthly numbers compound into big yearly ones."},
{"q":"Which of these is a lagging indicator that often peaks after a recession has ended?","options":["The yield curve","The unemployment rate","PMI new orders","Weekly jobless claims"],"answer":1,"why":"Firms keep cutting jobs for a while after output bottoms, so unemployment usually peaks late. Stocks often rise before it improves."},
{"q":"The 2-year yield is 4.6% and the 10-year yield is 4.1%. What is the 2s10s spread and what does it signal?","options":["+0.5%, a normal curve","+8.7%, an extremely steep curve","−0.5%, an inverted curve and a late-cycle warning","0%, a perfectly flat curve"],"answer":2,"why":"10-year minus 2-year = 4.1% − 4.6% = −0.5%. An inverted curve has preceded most US recessions, with variable lags."},
{"q":"A company has $1,000 of sales, $600 of fixed costs and variable costs of 20% of sales. If sales fall 10%, what happens to profit?","options":["Falls 10%","Falls 20%","Falls 40%","Stays the same"],"answer":2,"why":"Profit goes from $200 to $900 − $600 − $180 = $120, a 40% fall. Fixed costs amplify changes in sales."},
{"q":"In which economic season do both stocks and high-quality bonds tend to struggle at the same time?","options":["Stagflation: growth falling, inflation rising","Goldilocks: growth rising, inflation falling","Deflationary bust: growth and inflation falling","Early recovery after rate cuts"],"answer":0,"why":"Rising inflation forces rates up, hurting bonds, while slowing growth hurts earnings. Cash, TIPS and commodities tend to hold up better."},
{"q":"A fictional index has EPS of $200 and a P/E of 20. EPS rises 5% but the P/E falls to 18. What happens to the index?","options":["It rises 5%","It falls about 5.5%","It falls 10%","It rises about 15%"],"answer":1,"why":"New level = 210 × 18 = 3,780 vs 4,000 before, a change of about −5.5%. A falling multiple can outweigh rising earnings."},
{"q":"Your morning report says stocks fell 1.2% on a hot CPI number. Your 60/40 portfolio is now 58/42 with a 5-point band. What does the framework say to do?","options":["Sell stocks before the next CPI report","Move everything into cash until inflation falls","Buy long bonds because yields went up","Nothing: you are inside the band, so note the news and keep the plan"],"answer":3,"why":"Step 5 asks if your plan requires action. A 2-point drift is inside the band, and headlines alone are not a reason to trade."}
]
}]});
